"Cyclic Damage - Uniaxial Damaged Elasticity under Load Reversals (hysteresis)
'<hr />
'<h3>Model</h3>
'Same uniaxial <b>damaged elasticity</b> law as the <i>Concrete Damage</i> example,
'but now the damage is a <b>persistent state</b> (historical maximum), so the concrete
'is driven through <b>load reversals</b>. On unloading and reloading the stress stays
'on the <b>secant</b> to the origin with the frozen damage, the stiffness degrades every
'cycle and the crack closes when the sign of the strain flips:
#math 360 44
\sigma = \bigl(1 - d\bigr)\,E\,\varepsilon,\qquad d = \max_{\text{history}} d(\varepsilon),\qquad d \in [0,1]
#end math
'Sign convention: tension <var>ε</var> &gt; 0 (positive), compression <var>ε</var> &lt; 0.
'This reproduces the secant (no residual plastic strain) cyclic response of
'<b>ASDConcrete1D</b> (ASDEA / STKO).

'<h3>Material properties (same as Concrete Damage)</h3>
'<table><tr><td>
'Elastic modulus -'E = 30000
'Compressive strength -'f_c = 30
'Peak compressive strain -'ε_c0 = 0.002
'Ultimate compressive strain -'ε_cu = 0.0035
'Tensile strength -'f_t = 3
'Tensile cracking strain -'ε_t0 = f_t/E
'Tension softening strain -'ε_tf = 0.0005
'</td></tr></table>
'Compression softening slope (residual 0.2·f_c reached at ε_cu):
Z = (1 - 0.2)/(ε_cu - ε_c0)

#hide
'--- Envelope constitutive law σ(ε) [MPa] (identical to Concrete Damage) ---
'Tension branch: elastic up to ε_t0, then exponential softening
σ_t(ε) = if(ε ≤ ε_t0; E*ε; f_t*exp(-(ε - ε_t0)/ε_tf))
'Compression branch (x = -ε is the magnitude): Hognestad/Kent-Park parabola
'up to the peak, then a linear descending branch with residual 0.2·f_c
σ_c(ε) = if(-ε ≤ ε_c0; -f_c*(2*(-ε/ε_c0) - (-ε/ε_c0)^2); -f_c*max(0.2; 1 - Z*(-ε - ε_c0)))
'Full envelope law by sign of ε
σ(ε) = if(ε ≥ 0; σ_t(ε); σ_c(ε))
'Secant damage of the envelope: d = clamp(1 - σ/(E·ε), 0, 1), with d = 0 at ε = 0
d(ε) = if(ε ≡ 0; 0; min(1; max(0; 1 - σ(ε)/(E*ε))))
#show

'<h3>State machine (secant unloading / reloading)</h3>
'Two history variables are carried through the loop and only ever <b>grow</b>:
'<var>ε_tmax</var> (largest tensile strain seen) and <var>ε_cmax</var> (largest
'compressive magnitude seen). At every step the active branch uses its own frozen
'damage, so the response returns to the origin along the secant <var>(1 − d)E</var>:
#math 360 48
\varepsilon \ge 0:\;\sigma=(1-d(\varepsilon_{tmax}))E\varepsilon\quad\quad\varepsilon<0:\;\sigma=(1-d(-\varepsilon_{cmax}))E\varepsilon
#end math

'<h3>Strain protocol (reversals with growing amplitude)</h3>
'Piecewise-linear path through the corner strains below (compression cycles of
'increasing amplitude, unloaded to zero each time, plus one tension excursion):
#math 360 24
0 \to -0.0006 \to 0 \to -0.0012 \to 0 \to -0.0020 \to 0 \to -0.0030 \to 0 \to +0.0001 \to 0 \to -0.0040
#end math
np = 12
x_c = [0; -0.0006; 0; -0.0012; 0; -0.0020; 0; -0.0030; 0; 0.0001; 0; -0.0040]
'Sub-steps per segment -'sub = 40
'Total steps -'N = (np - 1)*sub + 1

"Cyclic sweep with a loop (fills ε, σ and damage vectors)
'The outer loop walks the corners; the inner loop interpolates each segment. The
'history variables ε_tmax and ε_cmax are updated with max() and persist between
'steps, freezing the damage so unloading/reloading trace secant loops.
εv = vector(N)
σv = vector(N)
dtv = vector(N)
dcv = vector(N)
nv = vector(N)
ε_tmax = 0
ε_cmax = 0
#hide
εv.1 = x_c.1
σv.1 = 0
nv.1 = 1
#for s = 1 : np - 1
    #for j = 1 : sub
        idx = (s - 1)*sub + j + 1
        ε = x_c.s + (x_c.(s + 1) - x_c.s)*(j/sub)
        ε_tmax = max(ε_tmax; ε)
        ε_cmax = max(ε_cmax; -ε)
        εv.idx = ε
        σv.idx = if(ε ≥ 0; (1 - d(ε_tmax))*E*ε; (1 - d(-ε_cmax))*E*ε)
        dtv.idx = d(ε_tmax)
        dcv.idx = d(-ε_cmax)
        nv.idx = idx
    #loop
#loop
#show
'Peak compressive stress reached by the sweep -'min(σv)' MPa
'Maximum compression damage (persistent) -'max(dcv)
'Final compression damage at ε = -0.0040 -'dcv.N

"Envelope peak values (validated)
'On first loading to each amplitude the state lands exactly on the envelope, so the
'peaks match the validated Concrete Damage table.
'<table style="border-collapse:collapse" border="1" cellpadding="4">
'<tr><th>ε (peak)</th><th>σ (MPa)</th><th>d</th></tr>
'<tr><td>-0.00060</td><td>'σ(-0.0006)'</td><td>'d(-0.0006)'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>-0.00120</td><td>'σ(-0.0012)'</td><td>'d(-0.0012)'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>-0.00200 (peak f_c)</td><td>'σ(-0.002)'</td><td>'d(-0.002)'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>-0.00300</td><td>'σ(-0.003)'</td><td>'d(-0.003)'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>-0.00400 (residual)</td><td>'σ(-0.004)'</td><td>'d(-0.004)'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>+0.00010 (tension)</td><td>'σ(0.0001)'</td><td>'d(0.0001)'</td></tr>
'</table>

"Hysteresis evidence (secant vs envelope at the same strain)
'After the first cycle the damage is frozen, so at ε = -0.0003 the reloading stress
'differs from the monotonic envelope — proof that the path traces a loop, not the
'backbone. With sub = 40 the first peak (-0.0006) sits at index 41 and the point
'reloaded at -0.0003 from that cycle sits at index 61.
'<table style="border-collapse:collapse" border="1" cellpadding="4">
'<tr><th></th><th>ε</th><th>σ (MPa)</th></tr>
'<tr><td>1st peak (index 41)</td><td>'εv.41'</td><td>'σv.41'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>secant unload (index 61)</td><td>'εv.61'</td><td>'σv.61'</td></tr>
'<tr><td>monotonic envelope</td><td>-0.0003</td><td>'σ(-0.0003)'</td></tr>
'</table>
'The secant stress -'σv.61' MPa (frozen d = 0.15) is flatter than the envelope
'-'σ(-0.0003)' MPa: the unloading branch degrades away from the backbone.

PlotWidth = 500
PlotHeight = 300
#post
"Hysteresis  σ vs ε
'Parametric plot of the whole protocol. Each cycle unloads to the origin along the
'secant (1 − d)E and reloads on the same line until it rejoins the envelope; the
'slope flattens every cycle as the stored damage grows — the hysteresis loops.
$Plot{εv.k | σv.k @ k = 1 : N}
"Compression-damage growth  d_c
'The persistent compression damage only increases through the protocol: it steps up
'0 → 0.15 → 0.30 → 0.50 → 0.844 → 0.95, staying flat during every unloading.
$Plot{nv.k | dcv.k @ k = 1 : N}
